        content: "<h3>The Internet is Just Computers Talking</h3><p>When you type <code>google.com</code> a DNS server translates the name to an IP address. Your browser opens a TCP connection, sends an HTTP request, and the server sends back HTML, CSS, and JS files.</p><h4>Key Concepts</h4><ul><li><strong>DNS</strong> — domain name → IP address lookup</li><li><strong>TCP/IP</strong> — rules for reliable data delivery</li><li><strong>HTTP/HTTPS</strong> — text protocol for transferring web pages</li><li><strong>Client vs Server</strong> — browser asks, server answers</li></ul><h4>The Browser's Job</h4><p>It parses HTML (DOM tree), applies CSS (CSSOM), runs JavaScript, then paints pixels. This is called the <strong>critical rendering path</strong>. Understanding it helps you build faster pages.</p>",
